the taliban has captured its eighth  provincial capital in afghanistan this  time in the city of pali khumri the  capital of baghlan province which is  just north of kabul  earlier fighters overran the city of  farah in the south west after a fierce  gun battle afghan forces also battling  the taliban to save the provinces of  helmand kandahar and harat but they are  struggling to stop the armed groups  rapid advance as u.s troops complete  their pull out by the end of this month  let's get more from rob mcbride now who  joins us live on the phone from kabul so  robert worrying another worrying  development this uh for the afghan  government forces giving this particular  city is is quite close to uh kabul  that's right this is a blow for the  afghan government it is as you say it is  the eighth province now to fall uh in uh  a short number of days there has been  fighting in this province for the past  couple of days uh and the uh security  forces in poly cumbria have come under  pressure uh and it was claimed earlier  by the taliban that they'd forced these  security forces out that has now been  confirmed to us that in fact this has  become the latest provincial capital to  fall to the taliban it is one of the uh  the less important provincial capitals  uh  there have been a number of smaller  cities that have fallen it is it's still  in this uh northern swathe of the  countryside that has seen a lot of the  fighting but nonetheless it does bring  that fighting closer to kabul and that  must be the concern for of the  government uh that we have seen so much  uh fighting really across the whole uh  the whole of afghanistan uh in the past  week or so in fact around half of all of  the provinces are reporting some sort of  conflict or clashes  of different degrees so this is the  latest uh province to fall as far as the  government is concerned its priority now  is to concentrate on some of the more  important provincial centers uh such as  mazari sharif and herac  centers that it does certainly still  have control of and put its resources  there it says it does have plans to  retake some of these lesser provinces  but given the pace of the advanced by  the taliban it remains to be seen if  it's going to put those counter-affect  those counter-attacks into effect uh and  if indeed they will be successful  rob thanks for that update rob mcbride  there on the line from kabul for us well  the setback for the afghan government  comes just hours after emergency talks  began here in doha to stop the violence  the top u.s diplomat for afghanistan is  in the  qatari capital for those discussions  we'll  have  the latest on the fighting in a moment  but first let's go to muhammad jamjum in  dalha for more  on those talks muhammad what do you know  well hasim the talks have officially  ended for this the first day of these  emergency talks here in doha  just in the past 45 minutes or so  we saw u.s special envoy for  afghanistan's al-meghalzad  as well as members of the taliban  delegation leaving the conference center  just behind us here at this venue they  did not make any remarks to the  assembled press and that's really been a  theme throughout the day many of the  diplomats here just not speaking on the  record or on camera throughout the day  really waiting to see what happens as a  result of these talks there is a great  push here by many international actors  uh throughout the day you've had the  envoys from qatar from the uk u.n eu  china pakistan and uzbekistan as well of  course as the u.s they've been here they  met first with a delegation from afghans  from the afghanistan government led by  dr abdullah abdullah head of the  national reconciliation council of  afghanistan um that meeting lasted just  about an hour and a half and then a  couple of hours after that you had a  much longer meeting uh which was was  between these envoys that are here and  the members of the taliban delegation  again unclear what exactly was said  during those meetings but if you take a  step back and look at a statement that  was issued by u.s special envoy  envoy  khalilzad earlier today he said the  following he said in several planned  rounds of meetings over three days  representatives from countries in the  region and beyond as well as from  multilateral organizations will press  for a reduction of violence and  ceasefire and a commitment not to  recognize a government imposed by force  so it certainly seems to indicate that  one of the messages that was perhaps  conveyed to members of the taliban  delegation would have been that the  international actors assembled here are  not going to  recognize any government in afghanistan  that is seized by force seized by the  taliban by force  where exactly this leads to next is  anybody's guess one of the things that  the diplomats that are assembled here  would like to see happen is they would  like to see the intra-afghan dialogue  the inter-afghan peace process which has  been playing out here in doha since last  september the last meeting of which  happened in mid-july they'd like to see  that get back on track  some have suggested throughout the day  that perhaps maybe an announcement could  be made before the end of these meetings  that another intra-afghan peace talk  session might happen but again that's  all just speculation at this point  really what these international actors  want to see happen is they want to come  up with some sort of joint plan to try  to get things back on track in  afghanistan to try to come up with some  sort of joint plan to respond to a very  rapidly deteriorating situation in  afghanistan hazard all right for the  moment uh muhammad jam jum lifers there  in darha thanks mohammed  now the european union says about 400  000 afghans have been internally  displaced by the fighting in recent  months as more provincial capitals fall  to the taliban thousands have fled to  kabul but as charlotte bellis reports  from the afghan capital they have  arrived to find little support  thousands of people have arrived at this  kabul park the majority have come from  northern afghanistan predominantly the  provinces of taha and kunduz both of  which their provincial capitals fell to  the taliban over the weekend people here  tell us that they were bombarded with  airstrikes there was constant gunfire  and they struggled to find a way out  they were firing one bullet but the  government forces were firing hundreds  of them over our heads then i said to my  wife let's leave everything and just run  our area is destroyed people were  running past dead bodies and not  stopping  people here have told us it was a real  challenge even just to reach kabul they  really struggled to find transport to  get them from the north they had to take  the seats out of buses to pack more  people in they said it took them about  10 to 12 hours to reach kabul and that  they had to cross through badland where  there is a big battle between the  government and taliban  when i was leaving the city i noticed  the families were gone from their homes  they locked their doors there were women  on the streets running and there were  lots of airstrikes i saw one woman who  was hit and injured maybe with a mortar  when i saw that i cried for hours  when the taliban came they looted  everything they burned our houses we  just ran we left our clothes and left  our front door open and just ran  now we live in this tents please help us  the government is offering some handouts  people in kabul are offering donations  also but as you can see there is very  little support shelter food or aid for  the people arriving here now the un says  this situation is not unique they say  that in the first week of august they  have counted 70 000 people who have fled  their homes due to conflict and are in  need of their support they also say 30  000 people are fleeing the country every  week charlotte ballas al jazeera kabul
 

